[{:db/id "q-357604/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "77918e45-4ef8-5cea-b2a1-7caf6b2d0374" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-357604/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "010b7ff4-8da8-5cb8-a6cb-22f5764d17d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-357604/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "bad3c6a9-1a65-558a-a134-1e6436f63a23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-357604/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-357604/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-357604/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-357604" :question/id #uuid "0515c2b0-200c-537d-a109-7c167dcd9b0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-357604" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/9542a7ddac8932b40e6f3a4217e7d97af6d1f3fc2598c595f9661ef5bc2b98ee.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is given by the following integral expression below: \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{a}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2)\\text{d}x\\,-{\\int }_{a}^{3}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2} & =2 \\\\ 12 & =2({x}^{2}+2) \\\\ 12 & =2{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ 8 & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ 4 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\pm 2 & =x \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=1$ and $x=3$ is $x=2.$\n\nSince $2\\in [1,3],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=3.$\n\nSo, $a=2$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2)\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{2}^{3}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-357604/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18cd52a9-1524-5099-b1d8-858345328bad"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-357604"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140584/selection/b8fe8d3cb42910de21f3df8634e1c373223a74d32bfef959cca9ceacd5c4d03e" :answer/id #uuid "78ee66c6-230a-5871-b2c6-6d1bbd0f82ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140584/selection/843cac81a39ec420eeda5b659ca0c95fca768adb954c51387f58e06c289a039c" :answer/id #uuid "d2d0dadb-6214-5bc1-8fe4-f535c93ead35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140584/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "bd06ac87-5e69-5db1-b493-cb49888bf47b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140584/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "5af3c3b1-59e0-58f4-9619-f40bacdcbeb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140584/selection/8cf4edd6abdb88922c00414679288e866f4f3f0653d5d908a8c994f8f6e02d92" :answer/id #uuid "786a3a15-d2ae-54b6-86f4-a2475e890a32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140584/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c42d4844-669d-55e2-b2b6-2f76493dabf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140584/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-140584/selection/b8fe8d3cb42910de21f3df8634e1c373223a74d32bfef959cca9ceacd5c4d03e" "q-140584/selection/843cac81a39ec420eeda5b659ca0c95fca768adb954c51387f58e06c289a039c" "q-140584/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-140584/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-140584/selection/8cf4edd6abdb88922c00414679288e866f4f3f0653d5d908a8c994f8f6e02d92"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140584" :question/id #uuid "aadedb3c-0394-5e6a-9376-0f11360cc534" :question/math-academy-id "q-140584" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/7d49ecfb67ace2bf8fa93b44c24796bbcc9953b3ee9976d30c9e50e28f01ff04.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-5$ to $x=-1$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-5}^{a}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{-1}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{4} & =16 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{(x+2)^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{16} \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{{2}^{4}} \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=-5$ and $x=-1$ is $x=-4.$\n\nSince $-4\\in [-5,-1],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-5$ to $x=-4.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-4$ to $x=-1.$\n\nSo, $a=-4$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-5}^{-4}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140584/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18cd52a9-1524-5099-b1d8-858345328bad"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140584"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140579/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer/id #uuid "0fd02b0c-2c89-54bf-a9c0-6b6729b8b3f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140579/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "e6dd313d-6b50-5de2-b98c-d4207a35cda0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140579/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "d3648aa9-4bc3-5368-b04c-873d9b559e10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140579/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "954bc3b7-3072-53c5-83ee-8a813ad6168f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140579/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "75ebf884-3239-5c39-9f9f-68e6e4f50941" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140579/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f8e0f3f-7887-5002-a7fa-43877ede7b4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140579/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-140579/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" "q-140579/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-140579/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-140579/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-140579/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140579" :question/id #uuid "32164094-a2a0-521c-8478-fb3407c48f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140579" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/a71e2e5e4cd2fddac779a7c2d21d072663f406bc6595ef9c7e15c885bbb83889.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=1-\\frac{4}{x+3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=5$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-2}^{a}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{a}^{5}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=1-\\frac{4}{x+3}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{4}{x+3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{x+3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{x+3} & =1 \\\\ 4 & =x+3 \\\\ 1 & =x \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\in [-2,5],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=5.$\n\nSo, $a=1$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140579/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18cd52a9-1524-5099-b1d8-858345328bad"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140579"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140574/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "5e5cf55b-f6ce-5d74-b427-7c8e0fe66511" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140574/selection/792e4603c3cdfaae400be056328702ef30f8064851fe81d040710db9424c8ade" :answer/id #uuid "086d8ca0-cbdc-5dca-8f31-895b3d1a8359" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140574/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer/id #uuid "3b100573-d2d3-54cb-922a-e9d84f3a3b5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140574/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "68da8c81-7799-5768-aab5-40553f7393f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140574/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "f94e74d7-6506-5ec2-a552-3a4a54e5b70a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140574/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ecaa2833-36af-50ba-8a7c-0b7bae9b74ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140574/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-140574/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-140574/selection/792e4603c3cdfaae400be056328702ef30f8064851fe81d040710db9424c8ade" "q-140574/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" "q-140574/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" "q-140574/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140574" :question/id #uuid "e12337e4-5c20-5826-a0ae-2851df2fd5de" :question/math-academy-id "q-140574" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/41e7b3f6588f7f4dd55096fb95bbe517a053c11f80c43aab171d80829864d2c3.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=3x-2{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=\\sqrt{10}$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{a}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{10}}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=3x-2{x}^{2}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(3-2x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{2}\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ to $x=\\sqrt{10}.$\n\nSo, $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{3/2}^{\\sqrt{10}}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-140574/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18cd52a9-1524-5099-b1d8-858345328bad"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140574"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-316137/field-1/85aa965640aba9bcb1eb584cf5dd96ebd6aa564058946250528a7660b311a583" :answer/id #uuid "3da09d2a-2866-5a22-80b8-26123454dd72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-316137/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe99b8ad-e244-5cdf-917e-4bfb477345c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-316137/field-1/85aa965640aba9bcb1eb584cf5dd96ebd6aa564058946250528a7660b311a583" :answer-field/choices ["q-316137/field-1/85aa965640aba9bcb1eb584cf5dd96ebd6aa564058946250528a7660b311a583"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-316137" :question/id #uuid "1e7948d4-01cb-55e2-bf38-1f985cf8e7f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-316137" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/3aa68477e9e8f3bc688981cbd227e21af798364b8c0334ee9a22163b400b4064.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0$ is given by the following integral expression below: \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-2}^{a}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{a}^{0}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{4} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{(x-1)^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{9} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm {9}^{1/4}\\,=\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=-2$ and $x=0$ is $x=1-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSince $1-\\sqrt{3}\\in [-2,0],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1-\\sqrt{3}$ to $x=0.$\n\nSo, $a=1-\\sqrt{3}$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-2}^{1-\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-316137/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18cd52a9-1524-5099-b1d8-858345328bad"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-316137"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-54476"] :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is our only real solution. Since $-1\\in [-2,1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/b0f2899bf6a946f6d7bf8bc60c41827bd05aef500f0fc0f30a5916c26b1007bb.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-2,-1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{4}{|}_{-2}^{-1}-4x{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-[(-1)^{4}-(-2)^{4}]-4[-1+2] \\\\  & =-(-15)-4 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,1]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{1}+4x{|}_{-1}^{-1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{4}-(-1)^{4}]+4[1-(-1)] \\\\  & =0+8 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=11+8=19.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-117442/selection/eb57fb392c21478621c4c209554229c785123f38918aca582a228a144fdce87a" :answer/id #uuid "93a01e6b-3e82-52b3-bd11-db04f73363da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117442/selection/5532846bc1afee77d13b0a7fba650aee23d823b7b49e6b15c0689e407dbb71c9" :answer/id #uuid "8ef2b41f-97a2-5f1d-b26a-22329b7046db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{75}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117442/selection/23a7b929cccc5bdeb88db3bdc770afbaf59570d63e930fc5c94f26cb6f5091dc" :answer/id #uuid "d778ba44-bd84-5a42-9a96-0564cdcd85b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117442/selection/0086db6077a62a5e2d9c771f7cdf57bea92b1de496b4d9b1d9c50dc1e5b58991" :answer/id #uuid "fee7b23d-f66a-5d3c-8805-a28405f0b85c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "70" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117442/selection/739adb08fce63c689cb03e270b8af2988607c3e1da7bbff8d5151160d544f6e4" :answer/id #uuid "7b85d57d-175c-5ba4-b24e-1424ddd2a18e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{75}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117442/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "020892ed-2d12-5055-b5d4-7d7513fe916a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117442/selection/5532846bc1afee77d13b0a7fba650aee23d823b7b49e6b15c0689e407dbb71c9" :answer-field/choices ["q-117442/selection/eb57fb392c21478621c4c209554229c785123f38918aca582a228a144fdce87a" "q-117442/selection/5532846bc1afee77d13b0a7fba650aee23d823b7b49e6b15c0689e407dbb71c9" "q-117442/selection/23a7b929cccc5bdeb88db3bdc770afbaf59570d63e930fc5c94f26cb6f5091dc" "q-117442/selection/0086db6077a62a5e2d9c771f7cdf57bea92b1de496b4d9b1d9c50dc1e5b58991" "q-117442/selection/739adb08fce63c689cb03e270b8af2988607c3e1da7bbff8d5151160d544f6e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117442" :question/id #uuid "8436e1e6-e8bd-5956-8eec-60c70d16a98c" :question/math-academy-id "q-117442" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3{x}^{3}+24$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+24 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{3} & =-24 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-2\\in [-3,-1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/80315b4862adf8ebb1f91f5c4c7606a59dfa5738c40e34179a745e03a0a25693.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-3,-2]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}({x}^{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+8x)|}_{-3}^{-2} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot ([4-16]-[\\frac{81}{4}-24]) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-12+\\frac{15}{4}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{33}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{99}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-2,-1],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({x}^{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+8x)|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot ([\\frac{1}{4}-8]-[4-16]) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{31}{4}+12) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{17}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{99}{4}+\\frac{51}{4}=\\frac{75}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-117442/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "262a1469-0920-565f-9268-28f0e5f1faf5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117442"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-54494"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/47e15993f3957fb3c1d7619fcf62544b2d5b358715538e17b9232dec5a0172c7.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2$, as shown above." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-140607"] :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/219d7aa5b976c97d47a96d0c94c3d61d139d5ddddc55b82d0df68f19ad6d5149.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [3,4]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{3}^{2}-{1}^{2}]-\\frac{1}{3}[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[9-1]-\\frac{1}{3}[27-1] \\\\  & =12-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [3,4],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{3}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{3}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[{4}^{2}-{3}^{2}]+\\frac{1}{3}[{4}^{3}-{3}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[16-9]+\\frac{1}{3}[64-27] \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}+\\frac{37}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{10}{3}+\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{31}{6}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-361717/field-1/1d578424150ed5243c71853988fdbe9e3a6438c4a964e75fbd7db9d570ed94ba" :answer/id #uuid "9e163693-e45a-535e-8720-315a6ad5e01b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{51}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-361717/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "49afcbe2-702c-5b9f-b605-3b5986796108" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-361717/field-1/1d578424150ed5243c71853988fdbe9e3a6438c4a964e75fbd7db9d570ed94ba" :answer-field/choices ["q-361717/field-1/1d578424150ed5243c71853988fdbe9e3a6438c4a964e75fbd7db9d570ed94ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-361717" :question/id #uuid "16b11549-efe0-5aac-94e8-57346bc60de5" :question/math-academy-id "q-361717" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/fc09b3b302a0920562a3ca03f52a2f6f98dc327b89f231e07b8a6ab631a48b2b.png)\n\nFind the total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=4,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=4.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [1,3]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{3}+{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[{3}^{4}-{1}^{4}]+[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[81-1]+[27-1] \\\\  & =-20+26 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [3,4],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{3}^{4}-{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{4}^{4}-{3}^{4}]-[{4}^{3}-{3}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[256-81]-[64-27] \\\\  & =\\frac{175}{4}-37 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{27}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{4}+\\frac{27}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-361717/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "262a1469-0920-565f-9268-28f0e5f1faf5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-361717"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-140577"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/a4871287696f20009bd7d2b8793965c9f68e35faa4ad150477643f1d3ec77e38.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-117447"] :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $3\\in [-3,5]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/6b3ca7be487585b296aaeb0b3fa7eb4ce8b9b4f9ad35d52baefa04f2fbbed621.png)\n\nFor $x\\in [-3,3]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27x{|}_{-3}^{3}-{x}^{3}{|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =27[3-(-3)]-[(3)^{3}-(-3)^{3}] \\\\  & =162-54 \\\\  & =108.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [3,5]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{5}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{5}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-27{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{3}^{5}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-27x{|}_{3}^{5}+{x}^{3}{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =-27[5-3]+[(5)^{3}-(3)^{3}] \\\\  & =-54+98 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=108+44=152.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-140580"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/2a32d95d98a2c2cefe08b492c368a99df61a8cc3a1f66018a6dfe444e65146b8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y=9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-140570/selection/7215d000b87deac87ee709a7bb39c8bd71aebbacbbdac9042d43673000907d31" :answer/id #uuid "5dd9c82f-233a-569d-b8e4-bbc1bf365aac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140570/selection/2d92eb8d863635b17cf8e72ff15ce179442a6c62acfbce41a3487bc7093e9288" :answer/id #uuid "197be805-58f7-5a01-a426-a204daba922c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140570/selection/a36a6fdf72fed515156c0ab26ea46d44cb23ef42d4cbf3f29e80ad74adca6b8c" :answer/id #uuid "7ad6f814-d795-56ae-aab1-0e2a8fcf077c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140570/selection/8d912e357e81ba93de061a6294f8bb89f61666dd6c2af9bcf272b0057b13f86c" :answer/id #uuid "bebecef8-fcb9-5315-82bf-df034a6dc5fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140570/selection/913b0b50abc8a297240f1bc2b6d0449b0096d7ab333edc44093825b9f07776c3" :answer/id #uuid "69c7b97a-8bfb-57c1-a868-dbb543937c93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140570/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb656972-1429-53aa-9365-77a1faf2084e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140570/selection/2d92eb8d863635b17cf8e72ff15ce179442a6c62acfbce41a3487bc7093e9288" :answer-field/choices ["q-140570/selection/7215d000b87deac87ee709a7bb39c8bd71aebbacbbdac9042d43673000907d31" "q-140570/selection/2d92eb8d863635b17cf8e72ff15ce179442a6c62acfbce41a3487bc7093e9288" "q-140570/selection/a36a6fdf72fed515156c0ab26ea46d44cb23ef42d4cbf3f29e80ad74adca6b8c" "q-140570/selection/8d912e357e81ba93de061a6294f8bb89f61666dd6c2af9bcf272b0057b13f86c" "q-140570/selection/913b0b50abc8a297240f1bc2b6d0449b0096d7ab333edc44093825b9f07776c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140570" :question/id #uuid "258bdd1d-0804-5b67-a22c-b1970d1d2fe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140570" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/8b7b81c97a842b82c2e4cbbd041883c1bd7f4f4ab58dc6592c12ec1cc0262620.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x(x+1)(x-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=-1,x=0$ or $x=3.$ Since $0\\in [-1,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-140570/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d89ba41-6d24-548a-bd97-66830b752ba9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140570"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-54482"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/a8a85024d555378ad7ba31262a290386777fa94b00208d6a68fdacb5bb61d3c8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{3}-x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3888"] :question/problem "The total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=x^3-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=2$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{a} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{a}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/57d01ea60e3cd9770f2ef978e46e2cef7b69a9fcdc2ac478d5b6c73d8f366c7b.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3887"] :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^3$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/0d3ede3cfbec765228ea184c74e0cb35f1df2a9340bd1cb703a0990249fecf30.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3889"] :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-9x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-9) & =0 \\\\ x(x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0,$ $x=-3,$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $0 \\in [-3,3]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/29a51c9892de59037e84bace2f8b5822222c3056a05af861cbd741390a437a61.png)\n\nFor $x \\in [-3,0]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}({x}^{3}-9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-9{\\int }_{-3}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-3}^{0}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{(-3)^{4}}{4}]-9[0-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x \\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{0}^{3} \\left(x^3-9x\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+9{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{(3)^{4}}{4}-0]+9[\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-0] \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 =  \\dfrac{81}{4} + \\dfrac{81}{4} = \\dfrac{81}{2}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
